      "rev_n_1_37a8db_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_37a8db_0",
        "node_id": "n_1_37a8db",
        "thought_text": "Simulate the process directly using a deque, appending to the right and then reversing (or swapping ends) for each step. This is O(n^2) due to repeated reversals, which will time out for n=2*10^5.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794507532.5719388
      },
      "rev_n_2_2a67f5_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_2a67f5_0",
        "node_id": "n_2_2a67f5",
        "thought_text": "Observe the pattern of element placement: odd-indexed elements (1-based) end up in the second half of the result in reverse order, while even-indexed elements end up in the first half in reverse order. Construct the result by iterating backwards through the input and placing elements based on their index parity.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794507532.5719526
      },
      "rev_n_3_c8b71a_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_c8b71a_0",
        "node_id": "n_3_c8b71a",
        "thought_text": "Use a double-ended queue (deque) to simulate the process efficiently. Instead of reversing the entire list, maintain a 'direction' flag. Append to the front or back depending on the flag, then flip the flag. This reduces the complexity to O(n).",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794507532.5719607
      },
      "rev_n_2_2a67f5_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_2a67f5_1",
        "node_id": "n_2_2a67f5",
        "thought_text": "The previous logic incorrectly mapped the parity of remaining steps to the left/right placement, causing the interleaving of elements to be reversed. The correct pattern is that elements at even indices (0-based) from the input should be placed in the first half of the result in reverse order, while elements at odd indices should be placed in the second half in reverse order.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_2_2a67f5_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_6311bf76a1",
        "created_at": 1794507550.5107968
      },
      "rev_n_4_a5c11a_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_4_a5c11a_0",
        "node_id": "n_4_a5c11a",
        "thought_text": "Simulate the process using a deque and a direction flag to avoid O(n^2) reversals, achieving O(n) time complexity.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "53371562650790009",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794507661.059209
      },
      "rev_n_5_843c9a_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_5_843c9a_0",
        "node_id": "n_5_843c9a",
        "thought_text": "Observe the pattern of element placement: odd-indexed elements (1-based) go to the right half in reverse order, while even-indexed elements go to the left half in reverse order, then concatenate.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "53371562650790009",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
