        "thought_text": "Thought-3 (corrected): The simulation logic incorrectly assumes that toggling the append side (left/right) after every step correctly models the \"append then reverse\" operation. In reality, the \"end\" of the sequence where the new element is appended alternates between the physical left and right ends of the deque, but the direction of the sequence itself also flips, meaning the logical \"end\" is always the side we just appended to, and the next append must happen to the opposite physical side relative to the current logical orientation. The correct approach is to recognize that after $n$ operations, the sequence consists of elements at odd indices (1-based) in their original order followed by elements at even indices in reverse order if $n$ is odd, or vice versa if $n$ is even, which can be constructed directly in $O(n)$ without simulating reversals.",
